Subject: [PATCH] nvme-pci: try PCIe FLR when controller disable/shutdown hangs

The kernel upstream patch ("nvme-pci: try function level reset on
init failure", upstream 5b2c214a9594, 6.12.y backport bc255fec6fd7)
only attempts a PCIe Function Level Reset when the controller fails to
disable from inside nvme_pci_configure_admin_queue() (the reset_work
path).  However, nvme_disable_ctrl() is also called from
nvme_dev_disable(), which is the first thing nvme_timeout() does for a
hung admin or I/O command:

    nvme_timeout()
      -> nvme_dev_disable(dev, false)
           -> nvme_disable_ctrl(&dev->ctrl, shutdown)   <-- can hang
      -> nvme_try_sched_reset(&dev->ctrl)

When the controller is wedged this nvme_disable_ctrl() times out with

    nvme nvme0: Device not ready; aborting reset, CSTS=0x1

but nvme_dev_disable() discards the return value and proceeds to
pci_disable_device().  Depending on firmware behaviour the subsequent
reset_work may not reach the patched nvme_pci_configure_admin_queue()
site, because nvme_pci_enable() bails out earlier when
pci_enable_device_mem() fails or when CSTS reads back as -1.  In that
case the FLR fallback is never tried and the device is left needing a
power cycle.

Note on the 6.12 backport: v6.1 had a separate nvme_disable_admin_queue()
helper, and a separate nvme_shutdown_ctrl().  Both were removed upstream;
the helper body was inlined into nvme_dev_disable() and the shutdown
variant folded into nvme_disable_ctrl(ctrl, shutdown).  The equivalent
code is therefore the "if (!dead && dev->ctrl.queue_count > 0)" block of
nvme_dev_disable(), which is where this patch now applies.

Safety:
  - The block only runs when !dead, i.e. nvme_pci_ctrl_is_dead() found
    the device present, pci enabled, error_state normal, CSTS.CFS == 0
    and CSTS.RDY == 1 -- exactly the "stuck controller" case where FLR
    is appropriate.
  - pci_save_state() was already done by nvme_pci_enable(), so
    pci_restore_state() after FLR is correct.
  - After FLR the controller is in its reset state (CC == 0,
    CSTS.RDY == 0); the remainder of nvme_dev_disable() (poll irqdisable,
    suspend queues, pci_free_irq_vectors, pci_disable_device) is
    unaffected.
  - On a subsequent reset_work, nvme_pci_configure_admin_queue()'s
    nvme_disable_ctrl() will succeed immediately, so the upstream FLR
    path will not double-FLR.
  - nvme_dev_disable() returns void, so a failed FLR is only logged.

---
diff -Nur a/drivers/nvme/host/pci.c b/drivers/nvme/host/pci.c
--- a/drivers/nvme/host/pci.c
+++ b/drivers/nvme/host/pci.c
@@ -2787,9 +2787,31 @@
 	nvme_quiesce_io_queues(&dev->ctrl);
 
 	if (!dead && dev->ctrl.queue_count > 0) {
+		int result;
+
 		nvme_delete_io_queues(dev);
-		nvme_disable_ctrl(&dev->ctrl, shutdown);
+		result = nvme_disable_ctrl(&dev->ctrl, shutdown);
+		if (result < 0) {
+			/*
+			 * The controller did not respond to a normal disable or
+			 * shutdown. Use the lower level and bigger hammer PCIe
+			 * Function Level Reset to restore the device to its
+			 * initial state, so that a subsequent reset can succeed
+			 * without a power cycle.
+			 */
+			result = pcie_reset_flr(pdev, false);
+			if (result < 0) {
+				dev_warn(dev->ctrl.device,
+					 "pcie flr fallback failed (%d)\n",
+					 result);
+			} else {
+				pci_restore_state(pdev);
+				dev_info(dev->ctrl.device,
+					 "controller %s completed after pcie flr\n",
+					 shutdown ? "shutdown" : "disable");
+			}
+		}
 		nvme_poll_irqdisable(&dev->queues[0]);
 	}
 	nvme_suspend_io_queues(dev);
 	nvme_suspend_queue(dev, 0);
 	pci_free_irq_vectors(pdev);
-- 
2.34.1
